1
00:00:12,420 --> 00:00:15,390
Hey guys, welcome back to our IELTS Listening course.

2
00:00:16,570 --> 00:00:18,300
Let's take a look at these questions.

3
00:00:18,490 --> 00:00:19,850
What are they asking us to do?

4
00:00:22,740 --> 00:00:25,360
As you can see, they require details involving

5
00:00:25,360 --> 00:00:29,440
numbers, such as dates, prices, and quantities, or

6
00:00:29,440 --> 00:00:32,740
spelling, like phone numbers, names, and postal codes.

7
00:00:33,720 --> 00:00:35,380
Many of you think that these are easy.

8
00:00:35,380 --> 00:00:37,980
However, we are here today to make sure

9
00:00:37,980 --> 00:00:40,260
that you will never lose any points for

10
00:00:40,260 --> 00:00:41,280
this type of question.

11
00:00:42,600 --> 00:00:44,900
Today's lesson is divided into two parts.

12
00:00:45,520 --> 00:00:47,460
First, we will learn about how to listen

13
00:00:47,460 --> 00:00:48,740
for numbers on their own.

14
00:00:49,320 --> 00:00:51,320
And second, we will learn about how to

15
00:00:51,320 --> 00:00:53,780
handle combinations of numbers and letters.

16
00:00:54,340 --> 00:00:56,540
Without further ado, let's begin.

17
00:01:01,470 --> 00:01:03,370
Normally, you will be asked to listen to

18
00:01:03,370 --> 00:01:05,530
numbers in two main types of questions.

19
00:01:06,050 --> 00:01:10,790
First, completion tasks, like form completion in part 1,

20
00:01:10,790 --> 00:01:13,410
table or flowchart completion in part 2,

21
00:01:13,830 --> 00:01:16,010
and summary completion in part 4.

22
00:01:16,870 --> 00:01:20,410
Second, multiple choice questions with one correct answer

23
00:01:20,410 --> 00:01:23,170
in part 2, and some other rare cases

24
00:01:23,170 --> 00:01:25,030
in other parts of the listening test.

25
00:01:25,750 --> 00:01:27,130
So what is your task here?

26
00:01:27,390 --> 00:01:28,470
It is very simple.

27
00:01:29,070 --> 00:01:31,250
Listen to the recording and find the answers,

28
00:01:31,610 --> 00:01:37,330
which here are in the number form, such as 150 or 4:30 pm.

29
00:01:38,510 --> 00:01:40,970
Then the main challenge for this question type

30
00:01:40,970 --> 00:01:43,410
is simply how to listen to numbers correctly.

31
00:01:44,050 --> 00:01:46,350
So let's get to how to deal with this.

32
00:01:46,950 --> 00:01:48,590
For listening to numbers alone,

33
00:01:48,590 --> 00:01:50,190
there are two main scenarios.

34
00:01:50,870 --> 00:01:53,990
The easier scenario involves quantities or prices,

35
00:01:54,160 --> 00:01:55,790
where the answer is shorter.

36
00:01:56,010 --> 00:01:59,010
The harder scenario involves times and dates.

37
00:01:59,010 --> 00:02:02,040
To handle these scenarios perfectly,

38
00:02:02,040 --> 00:02:04,190
there are four things you need to understand

39
00:02:04,190 --> 00:02:07,250
and master how to write them in your answer sheet to

40
00:02:07,250 --> 00:02:09,770
prevent losing points in easy questions.

41
00:02:10,490 --> 00:02:11,990
1. Punctuation

42
00:02:11,990 --> 00:02:13,790
2. Currency

43
00:02:13,980 --> 00:02:15,900
3. Dates

44
00:02:16,190 --> 00:02:17,990
4. Time

45
00:02:17,990 --> 00:02:19,790
1. Punctuation

46
00:02:20,120 --> 00:02:22,780
In English, when separating thousands,

47
00:02:22,790 --> 00:02:25,390
you can use a comma or you don't have to

48
00:02:25,390 --> 00:02:28,030
write down any punctuation with the number at all.

49
00:02:28,750 --> 00:02:30,950
However, you should not put a full stop

50
00:02:30,950 --> 00:02:32,270
or an apostrophe here.

51
00:02:32,950 --> 00:02:36,110
For example, one thousand is written as this

52
00:02:36,110 --> 00:02:38,450
or this, not this.

53
00:02:39,390 --> 00:02:42,050
However, for decimal numbers, we use a full

54
00:02:42,050 --> 00:02:43,470
stop or a period.

55
00:02:44,370 --> 00:02:48,350
Four and a half or 4.5 is written as this.

56
00:02:49,010 --> 00:02:50,410
Never write this.

57
00:02:51,190 --> 00:02:55,130
Just remember, comma for thousands, dot for decimals.

58
00:02:55,850 --> 00:02:58,190
2. Currency

59
00:02:58,190 --> 00:03:01,190
Usually, you will hear dollars or pounds.

60
00:03:01,550 --> 00:03:04,310
Normally, the question will provide the symbol, so

61
00:03:04,310 --> 00:03:06,670
you must not write the currency symbol again.

62
00:03:07,630 --> 00:03:09,990
In rare cases, if the question does not

63
00:03:09,990 --> 00:03:12,770
provide the symbol, please remember that the symbol

64
00:03:12,770 --> 00:03:14,550
always goes before the number.

65
00:03:15,330 --> 00:03:17,630
And just like before, use a full stop

66
00:03:17,630 --> 00:03:18,390
for the cents.

67
00:03:18,860 --> 00:03:20,990
3. Dates

68
00:03:20,990 --> 00:03:23,290
This can be confusing because the format

69
00:03:23,290 --> 00:03:25,190
changes depending on the country.

70
00:03:25,730 --> 00:03:28,570
In the UK, they use day, month, year,

71
00:03:28,870 --> 00:03:30,330
like 18th May.

72
00:03:31,050 --> 00:03:34,290
In the US, they use month, day, year,

73
00:03:34,810 --> 00:03:36,570
like May 18th.

74
00:03:37,070 --> 00:03:41,390
The good news is that for IELTS, both formats are acceptable.

75
00:03:41,570 --> 00:03:43,620
However, remember to follow the word

76
00:03:43,620 --> 00:03:45,840
and number limits from the instructions.

77
00:03:46,190 --> 00:03:49,050
For instance, the 18th of May is counted

78
00:03:49,050 --> 00:03:51,050
as three words and one number.

79
00:03:51,790 --> 00:03:55,950
But 18th May or 18 May is just

80
00:03:55,950 --> 00:03:57,550
one word and one number.

81
00:03:59,570 --> 00:04:01,190
Also, when writing dates,

82
00:04:01,190 --> 00:04:03,590
you should always capitalize the month.

83
00:04:03,830 --> 00:04:07,250
Write April with a big A, never a small a.

84
00:04:10,000 --> 00:04:11,600
4. Time

85
00:04:11,990 --> 00:04:13,800
You can use either a colon or

86
00:04:13,800 --> 00:04:16,279
a full stop to separate hours and minutes.

87
00:04:16,279 --> 00:04:19,000
7:00 can be 7.00

88
00:04:19,000 --> 00:04:20,480
or 7 o'clock.

89
00:04:21,260 --> 00:04:25,420
Also, if the recording mentions a.m or p.m like this,

90
00:04:25,420 --> 00:04:27,360
you must not include a.m

91
00:04:27,360 --> 00:04:29,820
because it is already mentioned in the question.

92
00:04:32,190 --> 00:04:35,490
Finally, we should not forget our friend, distractors.

93
00:04:36,250 --> 00:04:38,850
First, the recording might provide a wrong answer

94
00:04:38,850 --> 00:04:41,390
or an unselected option first,

95
00:04:41,390 --> 00:04:43,190
then the correct one after.

96
00:04:43,390 --> 00:04:46,540
In this case, all you need is to listen closely.

97
00:04:47,390 --> 00:04:50,990
Second, the recording might provide a seemingly right answer, 

98
00:04:50,990 --> 00:04:53,510
but then the speaker corrects it by themselves.

99
00:04:54,290 --> 00:04:55,610
This one is a bit trickier.

100
00:04:56,230 --> 00:04:59,090
For example, one could say, My phone number

101
00:04:59,090 --> 00:05:05,910
is 01234990 and then correct it by saying,

102
00:05:06,430 --> 00:05:08,990
My mistake, the last two numbers are 09,

103
00:05:09,570 --> 00:05:10,630
not 90.

104
00:05:11,510 --> 00:05:14,070
Here, you need to correct the number by yourself.

105
00:05:18,380 --> 00:05:20,720
Now, let's move to the second part.

106
00:05:20,990 --> 00:05:22,790
Listening to numbers and letters.

107
00:05:23,620 --> 00:05:27,620
Number combinations like postal codes, license plates, emails,

108
00:05:28,000 --> 00:05:31,700
and room or house numbers often mix digits and letters.

109
00:05:32,520 --> 00:05:34,380
The golden rule here is accuracy.

110
00:05:35,160 --> 00:05:37,380
Do not add hyphens or spaces 

111
00:05:37,380 --> 00:05:38,920
unless you clearly hear them.

112
00:05:39,340 --> 00:05:41,840
If you have mastered listening to numbers only,

113
00:05:42,320 --> 00:05:44,220
this part is not challenging at all.

114
00:05:44,220 --> 00:05:47,460
However, please be cautious of letters that sound

115
00:05:47,460 --> 00:05:50,040
very similar to numbers or to other letters.

116
00:05:50,820 --> 00:05:52,160
Watch out for these pairs.

117
00:05:52,160 --> 00:05:55,160
8, H and A

118
00:05:55,160 --> 00:05:58,160
E and I, B and P

119
00:05:58,790 --> 00:06:02,390
T and D, G and J

120
00:06:02,390 --> 00:06:04,060
M and N

121
00:06:04,190 --> 00:06:05,390
S and X

122
00:06:05,990 --> 00:06:08,820
Also, be careful with numbers that sound alike,

123
00:06:08,990 --> 00:06:13,540
specifically the "-teen" versus the "-ty", such as

124
00:06:13,540 --> 00:06:16,140
15 versus 50.

125
00:06:16,920 --> 00:06:19,720
But don't worry, we have already prepared many

126
00:06:19,720 --> 00:06:22,380
listening practice questions for you after this lesson

127
00:06:22,380 --> 00:06:24,620
to help you master these distinctions.

128
00:06:25,020 --> 00:06:26,300
That's it for today's lesson.

129
00:06:26,600 --> 00:06:29,240
Don't forget to do the exercises and practice

130
00:06:29,240 --> 00:06:30,740
what you've learned regularly.

131
00:06:31,240 --> 00:06:33,180
Goodbye and see you in the next lesson.

